  "body_html": "<h1>Von Neumann Minimax Theorem</h1>\n<p>Every finite two-player zero-sum matrix game has a value in mixed strategies:</p>\n<p>$$\\max_x \\min_j E_A(x,j) = \\min_y \\max_i E_A(i,y).$$</p>\n<p>Equivalently, there are optimal mixed strategies $x^\\ast$ and $y^\\ast$ and a\nvalue $v$ such that player I guarantees at least $v$ against every column and\nplayer II holds player I to at most $v$ against every row.</p>\n<p>Two proof routes attach to this theorem as separate proof-plan nodes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"math.minimax.minimax_from_loomis\" href=\"../../math/minimax/math_minimax_minimax_from_loomis.html\">Minimax via the All-Ones Specialization of Loomis</a> (<strong>selected</strong>): finite\n  minimax is the $B = \\mathbf{1}$ specialisation of the Loomis theorem\n  (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"math.minimax.loomis_theorem\" href=\"../../math/minimax/math_minimax_loomis_theorem.html\">Loomis Theorem (Positive B)</a>). Fully formalised in Lean\n  (<code>EconCSLib.StrategicGame.MinimaxLoomis</code>), which inlines the $B = \\mathbf{1}$\n  case of the Loomis induction\n  (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"math.minimax.loomis_induction_proof\" href=\"../../math/minimax/math_minimax_loomis_induction_proof.html\">Direct Induction Proof Of Loomis Theorem</a>) rather than first porting\n  the general positive-$B$ Loomis statement. The compactness/continuity\n  building blocks it relies on live in the core simplex layer\n  (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"math.simplex.pure\" href=\"../../math/simplex/math_simplex_pure.html\">Point Mass on the Standard Simplex</a>, <a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"math.simplex.continuity\" href=\"../../math/simplex/math_simplex_continuity.html\">Continuity on the Real Standard Simplex</a>,\n  <a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"math.simplex.bounded_by_value\" href=\"../../math/simplex/math_simplex_bounded_by_value.html\">Pointwise Bounds Are Simplex Bounds</a>) and the existence + weak-duality\n  step is <a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"game_theory.strategic_game.zero_sum.lam_mu_existence\" href=\"zero_sum/game_theory_strategic_game_zero_sum_lam_mu_existence.html\">Existence of Optimal Mixed Strategies (Loomis Foundations)</a>; weak duality\n  (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"game_theory.strategic_game.zero_sum.maximin_le_minimax\" href=\"zero_sum/game_theory_strategic_game_zero_sum_maximin_le_minimax.html\">Maximin is Bounded by Minimax</a>) supplies one direction of the\n  equality.</li>\n<li><a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"game_theory.strategic_game.zero_sum.dantzig_proof_spine\" href=\"zero_sum/game_theory_strategic_game_zero_sum_dantzig_proof_spine.html\">Dantzig Proof Spine for Minimax (Ordered-Field Route)</a> (<strong>candidate</strong>, ordered-field-generic):\n  a constructive simplex-style pivot proof that works over any linearly\n  ordered field. Most of its algebraic scaffolding is proved; the\n  simplex-termination step (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"game_theory.strategic_game.zero_sum.simplex_termination\" href=\"zero_sum/game_theory_strategic_game_zero_sum_simplex_termination.html\">Dantzig Simplex Termination (Open)</a>) is still\n  open.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Chapter 2, Thm. 2.3.1] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MathFoundationsGameTheory.pdf</code>. Von Neumann minimax theorem for finite zero-sum games.</li>\n</ul>",
  "proof_html": null,
